ACC-008 — Engineering Memory

Document ID: ACC-008
Title: Engineering Memory
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Memory, the persistent cognitive capability that preserves Engineering Understanding for future retrieval, evolution, and reuse.

Engineering Memory enables Atlas to maintain continuity of cognition across time by storing contextualized Engineering Understanding without converting it into permanent Engineering Knowledge.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all Engineering Understanding preserved within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Engineering Memory may contain:

Engineering Understanding
Supporting Engineering Evidence
Context
Assumptions
Constraints
Reasoning history
Decision history
Relationships
Confidence information
Provenance information

Engineering Memory does not represent long-term Engineering Knowledge.

3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Memory is the persistent preservation of Engineering Understanding, together with its supporting context, evidence, reasoning, and provenance, to enable future retrieval, refinement, and reuse.

Engineering Memory preserves cognition.

It does not create new cognition.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Memory SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Persistent

Engineering Memory SHALL preserve Engineering Understanding beyond the duration of a single reasoning process.

4.2 Context-Preserving

Engineering Memory SHALL retain the engineering context associated with preserved understanding.

4.3 Traceable

Engineering Memory SHALL preserve traceability to supporting Engineering Evidence, observations, and reasoning.

4.4 Retrievable

Engineering Memory SHALL support efficient retrieval of previously preserved understanding.

4.5 Evolvable

Engineering Memory SHALL support modification, refinement, and supersession as Engineering Understanding evolves.

4.6 Non-Authoritative

Engineering Memory SHALL preserve historical understanding without asserting that it remains correct.

4.7 Versioned

Engineering Memory SHOULD support versioning of preserved Engineering Understanding to maintain historical continuity and enable comparison across revisions.

5. Relationships

Engineering Memory preserves Engineering Understanding while supporting future cognition.

Engineering Understanding
        │
        ▼
Engineering Memory
        │
        ├──────────────► Engineering Understanding
        │                     ▲
        ▼                     │
Engineering Knowledge ◄────────┘

Engineering Memory:

Preserves Engineering Understanding.
Supports future Engineering Understanding.
Contributes to Engineering Knowledge through repeated validation.
Maintains engineering continuity.
6. Responsibilities

Engineering Memory is responsible for:

Preserving Engineering Understanding.
Retaining engineering context.
Maintaining evidence traceability.
Recording reasoning history.
Supporting retrieval and reuse.
Preserving historical evolution.
Supporting future reasoning processes.
7. Normative Requirements

Engineering Memory SHALL:

Preserve Engineering Understanding without altering its meaning.
Maintain links to supporting Engineering Evidence.
Preserve engineering context and provenance.
Support retrieval for future engineering cognition.
Record revisions when Engineering Understanding evolves.

Atlas SHALL NOT treat Engineering Memory as permanent Engineering Knowledge.

8. Non-Goals

Engineering Memory does not:

Define Engineering Reality.
Produce Engineering Evidence.
Perform Engineering Reasoning.
Replace Engineering Knowledge.
Guarantee correctness.
Represent immutable engineering facts.
9. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY define memory organization models, retention policies, indexing mechanisms, semantic retrieval strategies, and lifecycle management without changing the canonical definition established by this specification.